<p.1 id="Para-94">37.<i>Turner v. Turner</i>, 689 N.Y.S.2d 269 (3rd Dept., 1999). To understand the full
impact of that ruling, one should bear in mind that the majority of sex abuse
allegations are never substantiated sufficiently to result in a judicial finding of
abuse. The nature of the act makes legal proof unusually difficult. This ruling,
in other words, could well mean that a mother who alleges sex abuse@=#x2014;even if
her suspicions are understandable@=#x2014;stands a more than even chance of losing
custody.
</p.1>
<!--end of p1 paragraph-->


<!--enter p.1-->
<p.1 id="Para-95">38.The action by the appellate court was all the more striking because such courts
are generally highly reluctant to reverse child custody decisions, since these decisions often turn on subjective assessments of the parents, who appear before the
trial judge but not the appellate court.
</p.1>
