<p.1 id="Para-158">37.There is no record that the family court judge even forwarded these transcripts
to the appellate court, as required by New York law; New York Family Court Act
§ 664(b), Civil Practice Law &amp; Rules § 4019; see <i>Fleishman v. Walters</i>, 40 A.D.2d 622. In theory, an appellate court should not accept a family court custody ruling
if it is based more on private interviews than on evidence taken in court; <i>People
ex rel. Kessler v. Cotter</i>, 285 a.d. 206. In practice, appellate courts rarely give
family court decisions close scrutiny.
</p.1>
