<p.1 id="Para-184">64.Decision of May 1993. Despite the relatively cautious wording of this decision,
its real meaning was perfectly obvious to other judges who inherited the case.
An appellate judge would describe the earlier decision this way: <q>[The judge]
questioned her motivations, her credibility and her mental stability. It is clear
that he did not believe any part of plaintiff ’s allegations of abuse involving the
daughter. His findings were based upon a fully developed record following an
extensive evidentiary hearing.</q> (Memorandum decision, March 28, 1995.) This
confirms that the real <q>record</q> of a case like this is not the official legal one, for as
a matter of fact the abuse allegations had never been resolved by the court; they
were withdrawn when the father agreed to forgo all contact with the girl. Nor
did the earlier judge ever actually conclude that the charges had been false. The
<q>fully developed record</q> so calmly referred to by the appellate judge simply did
not exist in any legal sense. The <q>record</q> had been written, so to speak, between
the lines.
</p.1>
<!--end of p1 paragraph-->


