<p.1 id="Para-250">14.Transcript, November 20, 1996, pp 10@=#x2013;12. It is, alas, typical of family court procedure that an earlier judge had not hesitated to remove custody from the mother
in <i>her</i> absence@=#x2014;even though no evidence ever suggested the mother posed any
threat of harm to the children. But the mother could not get a court to consider
a temporary removal of custody from her ex-husband, even though there was an
imposing mass of evidence of abuse, <i>plus a doctor’s letter
indicating an emergency</i>, until all
attorneys@=#x2014;including the ex-husband’s attorney@=#x2014;were <q>prepared.</q>
